/P 87 0 R
/E (Cameras now serve as the "eyes" of smart factories, guiding robotic systems, ensuring product quality, and feeding processing engines with the real-time data needed for operational optimization. But this transformation has exposed critical limitations in traditional connectivity solutions. Currently, industrial machine vision is dominated by three legacy standards: GigE Vision, USB3 Vision, and CoaXPress. However, despite their dominance, each forces system designers into trade-offs between bandwidth, link distance, latency, reliability, complexity, and cost.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
110 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (USB3 Vision is a strong alternative where more bandwidth is required and longer link distance is not, but it also comes with a reputation of less reliability. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
111 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (GigE Vision, USB3 Vision, and CoaXPress each address part of the problem, but none addresses all of it. The gap between what legacy connectivity can deliver and what Industry 4.0 actually requires is widening, and it will only grow.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
112 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (In today's automated factories, every second counts, with every pixel captured potentially meaning the difference between flawless production and costly defects. Machine vision has evolved from a specialized quality control tool to the central nervous system of Industry 4.0 manufacturing. The global market is projected to grow at more than 7% annually over the coming years, according to Data Bridge Market Research, and is expected to reach nearly $20 billion by 2032 as manufacturers increasingly adopt AI-driven automation and quality control systems.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3 326 0 R 5]
>>
endobj
