251 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (Valens VA7000 takes the approach of using Pulse Amplitude Modulation \(PAM\) at the physical layer. This achieves about 90% link efficiency \(excluding error correction\) and is built to prioritize the mostly one-way direction that vision data actually moves. Additionally, its deserializer chips can combine signals from several cameras into a single connection that helps simplify wiring and keep camera streams in sync.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
252 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
253 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 94 0 R
/T (Enhanced Link Efficiency)
/E (Enhanced Link Efficiency)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
254 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 94 0 R
/T (Matching the Right Connectivity to How Vision Data Actually Flows)
/E (Matching the Right Connectivity to How Vision Data Actually Flows)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
255 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (Machine vision fundamentally operates by sending large volumes of image data from cameras to processors, with the return path carrying only smaller control and synchronization signals. As systems evolve and demand higher throughput for tasks like real-time quality control, the challenge is no longer just a matter of speed, but smarter use of resources.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
256 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (For machine vision systems running in factories, EMI is unavoidable. Copper cables, the backbone of industrial wiring, are prone to electromagnetic interferences from nearby heavy machinery, radio signals, or even walkie talkies resulting in critical link errors. Machine vision standards, like GigE Vision and CoaXPress, rely on Forward Error Correction \(FEC\) for handling errors. While FEC works well at handling low-level additive white Gaussian noise \(AWGN\), it becomes overwhelmed when confronted with sudden transient noise spikes, such as narrow-band interferences \(NBI\), which could result in burst errors.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
257 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 94 0 R
/T (Link Resilience Optimized for Industrial Cabling Infrastructures)
/E (Link Resilience Optimized for Industrial Cabling Infrastructures)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
258 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 94 0 R
/Pg 18 0 R
/A [579 0 R 0]
/K [9]
>>
endobj
259 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 95 0 R
/Pg 19 0 R
/A [580 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
260 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 95 0 R
/E (The VA7000 is designed specifically for these high-NBI industrial environments. Instead of relying solely on error correction, it employs two built-in technologies to keep connections reliable: localized retransmission at the physical layer and just-in-time noise cancellation. By resending lost packets automatically, VA7000 delivers uninterrupted, reliable communication even as machines turn on and off, or when increased electrical interference is present. The retransmission is time-bounded, and with MIPI A-PHY specification defining the latency limit to just 6uS @ 16Gbps.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
261 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 95 0 R
/E (The MIPI A-PHY ecosystem presently offers limited camera options compared to the three legacy standards. Many Industrial PC software vendors have not yet implemented native CSI-2 support, creating a barrier to integration with traditional IPC-based systems.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
262 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 95 0 R
/E (However, recent signs point to MIPI A-PHY ecosystem development acceleration as more companies express interest in automotive connectivity technologies as viable alternatives. Integration of Valens VA7000 chipsets into new products demonstrates the industry's willingness to embrace the new standard and the growing confidence in MIPI A-PHY's industrial potential.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
